WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.680
In the wild, animals affect each others behavior

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.680
all the time—prey species keep away from

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.020
predators’ turf, insects jump away from

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:11.880
browsing herbivores—they may not be communicating

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.280
per se but they shape each others’ environment.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.360
To further explore this relationship,

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.360
researchers used robots

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.000
to mediate between two species

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.640
unlikely to ever encounter each other in the wild:

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.540
zebrafish and honey bees.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.620
The researchers chose these species because

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.360
they are both social and gregarious animals

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.280
who regularly make decisions as a group.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.780
To do these experiments the animals were kept apart--

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.520
in fact, they are in different countries.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.940
In Switzerland, a small school of zebrafish

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.900
swim around a circular path,

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.380
with a camera overhead tracking their position.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.400
Swimming along with them is a robot lure,

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.480
the same size and shape as a zebrafish.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.180
Meanwhile in Austria, a swarm of young bees

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.440
buzz around an oblong enclosure, with two

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:58.400
robots in the middle.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.180
The bee-bots generate heat, which causes the bees

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.940
to congregate around them.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.320
A proximity detector tracks the bees’ location.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.940
In each setup, the fish and bees have a binary

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.500
collective choice to make - the bees can choose

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.920
to congregate around one of two robots

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.340
and the fish can swim either clockwise or

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.900
counterclockwise around the circle.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.820
The researchers wanted to know if,

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.820
using robots as mediators,

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:24.020
the two species could come to

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.580
a collective decision -

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:28.440
could the species agree which direction to swim in and

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.140
which bee-robot to gather around?

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.080
Four different experiments were performed

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.060
with varying degrees of communication

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:36.740
between the animal groups.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.500
The first experiment established a control group, with

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.420
no communication between the fish and the bees.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.860
In the second experiment, the bees’ behavior

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.780
was observed and then transmitted to the fish robot,

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:51.080
affecting which way it lured the school of zebrafish.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.540
After six minutes, the bees began to congregate

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.600
around the left bee robot, causing the fish robot

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:58.460
and, as a result, the fish

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:01.280
to swim in a counter clockwise direction.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:04.060
In the third experiment, the fish’ behavior was observed

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.220
and then transmitted to the bee robots,

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:08.860
affecting which robot produced heat.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:10.680
The fish constantly changed the direction

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:12.480
in which they were swimming, which caused

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.760
the bees to continuously switch sides as well.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.860
In the final experiment, the behavior of both

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:19.860
the bees and the fish was observed,

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.960
and transmitted both ways

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.080
At first, the fish and bees couldn’t come to a decision--

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:26.540
one side did one thing, the

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.040
other another, with lots of switching back and forth.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:33.060
But after about 20 minutes, they reached a consensus,

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.060
with the fish swimming counter-clockwise

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.080
and the bees congregating around the left bee-robot.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:40.760
This shows that two species, which would have no

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.980
interaction whatsoever in nature,

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:45.580
are actually able to interact and come to a collective

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.600
decision thanks to the help of the robots.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.620
Moving forward, researchers would like to introduce

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:53.460
machine learning into these types of scenarios

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.760
So bee-bots learn behavior from bees

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.260
and fish-bots from fish,

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.740
instead of being programmed ahead of time.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:01.520
Researchers believe that in the future,

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:03.900
this technology could be used to mediate species

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.060
interactions not just in the laboratory,

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.660
but also in the wild—the research could even be

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:12.540
adapted to work with plants, fungi and microorganisms

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.800
One day, machine-learning robots could be used

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.720
to perceive a dramatic event in a bee colony,

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:20.580
and react to help the bees to find a way to recover.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:23.260
This could be applied to any type of animal group, once

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.600
the robot knows how they make collective decisions.

